NAME
    eval - evaluate an integer expression against live core state

SYNOPSIS
    eval <expr>
    eval symbols [<filter>]
    eval <cpu> <expr>
    eval <cpu> symbols [<filter>]

DESCRIPTION
    Evaluates an integer expression against the attached core's live
    state and prints the result in decimal, hexadecimal, and grouped
    binary. The same expression language is used by conditional
    breakpoints and logpoints in bp(1).

    Prefixing a processor name from cpus(1) scopes register lookups and
    unqualified memory reads ('[addr]') to that processor and its code
    space. A processor name given alone without a following expression or
    'symbols' is evaluated as a symbol name (for example, 'eval a' reads
    register A on the main CPU).

OPTIONS
    symbols [<filter>]
        List all symbols available to expressions in the selected
        processor scope, optionally restricted to names containing
        <filter> (case-insensitive).

SYMBOLS AND UNITS
    'eval symbols' and 'eval <cpu> symbols' are the authoritative symbol
    lists for the loaded core. Common sources include:

        Registers   Every register reported by regs(1) for the target CPU,
                    plus system-pack aliases and flags (for example, on
                    SNES: a, x, y, s/sp, d/dp, pc, pb/pbr, db/dbr, p, e,
                    and flag.c/z/i/d/x/m/v/n).
        Context     frame, and where published: scanline, cycle, opaddr,
                    and stackdepth (call-stack depth while instrumentation
                    is armed).
        Labels      Every address name registered via label(1).
        Units       Hardware unit registers exposed by the core, always
                    addressable as '<unit>.<register>' (unit name
                    lowercased, non-alphanumeric characters stripped) and
                    also as '<register>' when unique across units and not
                    shadowed by a CPU register, system-pack symbol, or
                    label. Unit register reads are side-effect-free.

NUMBERS, MEMORY, AND OPERATORS
    Numbers     123 (decimal), $1F or 0x1F (hex), %1010 (binary).
    [addr]      Read one byte from the processor's default bus/space.
    {addr}      Read one little-endian 16-bit word.
    [SPACE:addr]
                Read from named memory space SPACE (for example,
                [VRAM:$2760]).

    On SNES, CPU-bus reads targeting WRAM read directly from RAM without
    hardware side effects; reads targeting hardware I/O registers go
    through the live bus and may trigger register side effects. Name a
    plain memory space explicitly when avoiding bus side effects.

    Operators from lowest to highest precedence:
        ||  &&  |  ^  &  == !=  < > <= >=  << >>  + -  * / %
    Unary operators: -  !  ~  and parentheses for grouping. '&&' and '||'
    short-circuit. Comparisons produce 1 or 0; any nonzero value is true.

    Quote expressions containing shell metacharacters ('&&', '||', '<',
    '>', '|'). Remaining arguments are joined with single spaces.

EXAMPLES
    eval a
    eval [$7E0020]
    eval {$7E13C6} + 4
    eval "flag.m == 0 && a > $100"
    eval [VRAM:$2760]
    eval symbols flag
    eval gsu symbols
    eval gsu r14
    eval spc "a == $F0"

EXIT STATUS
    0   Expression evaluated or symbol list printed (or usage printed).
    1   No target attached, parse/evaluation error, or session fault.

SEE ALSO
    cpus(1), bp(1), regs(1), mem(1), label(1), spaces(1)
